{"archetypes":[{"name":"stdout-protocol-drift","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan rewrites the Python dedup pass but the new test explicitly waives a removed-count assertion, leaving the stdout integer contract unverified after the two-pass restructure.","prompt_body":"The plan's two-pass Python rewrite must continue to print exactly one integer (the `removed` count) to stdout, consumed downstream by `${dedup_removed:-0}` in the shell wrapper. Scan the plan's proposed algorithm description for any path in pass 2 that could emit extra output (exception tracebacks, debug statements) or suppress the final count emission. Check whether the plan's testing strategy section explicitly validates this contract via the existing section-aware test, and assess whether the waived removed-count assertion in the new test case creates a verification gap. Also verify that the failure-modes section addresses what the shell wrapper observes if the Python block exits with an error before printing. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"awk-boundary-guard","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan imposes an unusual structural constraint on the Python heredoc (no top-level `}` line) to avoid awk truncation in tests, but does not verify that the proposed two-pass data structures can satisfy this constraint without layout-time drift.","prompt_body":"The plan explicitly warns against introducing a bare `}` at column zero inside `_run_post_apply_pipeline` because the test harness extracts the function with awk range `/^_run_post_apply_pipeline\\(\\)/,/^}$/`. Cross-check the current function's closing boundary in `plan-review-loop.sh` against the awk pattern used in `test-plan-review-loop.sh` to confirm the constraint is already load-bearing and not hypothetical. Evaluate whether the plan's described two-pass Python structure — a stack, a `set` named `in_fence_lines`, and a pass-2 index loop — can be written inside a heredoc without any line that is exactly `}` at column zero, or whether the plan should have prescribed a specific indentation or structure to guarantee this. Identify whether the plan provides actionable enough guidance to avoid this pitfall or leaves it as a silent implementation-time risk. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"test-edge-gap","focus_area":"architecture","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan documents seven distinct edge cases for the two-pass algorithm but the testing strategy mandates only one new regression test, leaving most edge cases without plan-anchored verification and creating coverage gaps the fix could silently regress.","prompt_body":"The plan's edge-cases section enumerates seven scenarios (empty plan, opener-as-last-line, multiple unbalanced openers, mixed balanced and unbalanced, language-tagged openers, mismatched-tick closers, and nested-looking fences) but the testing-strategy section mandates a single new test covering only the basic unclosed-fence/Constraints-protection path. Assess whether the plan explicitly accounts for which edge cases are intentionally excluded from the new test and whether the existing section-aware test incidentally exercises any of the seven. Pay particular attention to the nested-looking fence and mismatched-tick-closer edge cases, which are the most behaviorally distinct from the balanced-fence baseline and most likely to expose off-by-one errors in the stack logic. Check whether the plan's failure-modes section ties each failure mode to a detectable test signal or leaves regression detection implicit. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
